{"text": "Two\n(otober 12,2000\n\nCorporate Affairs\n\nGA: B&W To Close Macon Plant Temporarily\nBrown & Williamson announced on October 9\" that it\nwill close down its cigarette plant in Macon, Georgia\nfrom October 21-28 due to slow demand, The closure\nwill render about 2,000 employees idle during the\n\u201cdown days,\u201d according to company spokesman Jim\n\u2018Teague. The Macon plant produces KOOL, Carlton,\nLucky Strike, Capri and Misty brands. In September\n2000 the company offered early retirement packages to\n\naround 500 workers at the Macon and Louisville\nplants (AP 10/9)\n\n$1 Billion Credit For US Tobacco Co.\nGoldman Sachs and Bank of Nova Scotia have closed\n44 $1 billion credit for US Tobacco Company based in\nGreenwich, Connecticut, A banker close to the deal\nsaid the institutional lending has been oversubscribed\n(Securities Data Publishing 10/9).\n\nMarketing & Distribution\n\nNY Bans Herbal & Bidi Cigarette Sales\n\u2018New York Govemor George Pataki on October S\u00ae\nsigned into law two measures banning the sale of\nherbal cigarettes and the sale of bidis to minors.\n\u2018Violators who sell these two types of cigarettes to\nthose under age 18 will face the same fines and\npunishment as those who sell traditional tobacco\nproducts to minors. The law banning the sale of bidis\n\u2018comes into effect immediately, while the law on herbal\nCigaretes will tke effect on February 1, 2001 (AP\n1005).\n\nNY: New Tax Stamp To Fight Bootlegging\nTo fight cigarette bootlegging, State Tax\nCome ioner Arthur Roth announced that cigarette\npacks will henceforth have an apple-shaped red stamp\nin the city and a gold one in other parts ofthe State.\nThis is to distinguish them from those available at\ndifferent prices in the rest of the country (New York\nDaily News 10/8).\n\nNY: 19 Merchants Fined In Niagara County\n\u2018The Adolescent Tobacco Compliance Checks for 2000\nfound 19 merchants selling tobacco products to\nunderage customers in Niagara County. Second-time\noffenders Wilson Farms Nos. 39, 311 and 360 were\nfined $500 cach. First-time offenders received a $300\nfine each (Buffalo News 10/11).\n\nRJR0000001025020112\n70056 3436\n\nFIA Reiterates Ban; RJR Ends Winston 500\nFederation Intemnationale de I\u2019 Automobile (FIA), the\n\u2018motor sport's governing body, has reiterated its\ndecision to end tobacco sponsorship regardless of the\nEuropean Court of Justice dismissing the European\nUnion directive on tobacco advertising. An FIA\nspokesman said its decision should preempt alternative\nJaws and confusion, and that the ban would enable\nteams dependent on tobacco sponsorship to identify\nalternative sources by the end of the 2006 season. RJ\nReynolds Tobacco Co. also has announced the\n\u2018company's decision to end its Winston 500\nsponsorship after the race at Talladega Superspeedway\nwhile Sears terminated its 13-year association with\nTalladega after DieHard 500 in April (Associated\nNewspapers 10/6, AP 10/7).\n\nDC: ALA Wants PM Ads Off ABC\u2019s Website\nOn October 10\u201d in a letter to ABC News executives,\nCEO of the American Lung Association John Garrison\nasked that Philip Morris advertisements be removed\nfrom the health section of the abenews.com website,\n\u201cYou are certainly sending mixed, inappropriate\n\u2018messages to your audience,\u201d Garrison wrote to Eisner,\nABC News chairman Rone Arledge and other news\nexecutives (US Newswire 10/10).\n\ne9El sles", "words": [{"text": "Two", "x0": 659, "y0": 99, "x1": 698, "y1": 107, "conf": 0.0}, {"text": "(otober", "x0": 622, "y0": 111, "x1": 659, "y1": 119, "conf": 0.0}, {"text": "12,2000", "x0": 662, "y0": 111, "x1": 699, "y1": 121, "conf": 88.0}, {"text": "Corporate", "x0": 300, "y0": 190, "x1": 401, "y1": 214, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "Affairs", "x0": 406, "y0": 190, "x1": 473, "y1": 208, "conf": 95.0}, {"text": "GA:", "x0": 74, "y0": 216, "x1": 97, "y1": 227, "conf": 95.0}, {"text": "B&W", "x0": 102, "y0": 216, "x1": 135, "y1